STUDY_MATERIALS = [
    ("Python Programming", "Variables and data types", "Store values with clear names. Common types include int, float, str, bool, list, and dict.", "Variables point to values. Use type() to inspect a value. A list is ordered and mutable; a tuple is ordered and immutable; a dictionary maps keys to values.", "name = 'Maya'\nage = 20\nprint(name, age, type(age).__name__)", "Maya 20 int", "Mixing strings and numbers without conversion; changing a list when a tuple would be safer. Tip: choose descriptive names and validate input.", "1. Which type stores key-value pairs? Answer: dict.\n2. Is a string mutable? Answer: No, strings are immutable.", "Values have types; names make code readable; collections hold related data."),
    ("Python Programming", "Conditions and loops", "Conditions choose a path, while loops repeat work until a condition changes.", "if/elif/else handles choices. for iterates over a sequence. range(3) produces 0, 1, 2. Use break carefully and keep loop conditions finite.", "for number in range(1, 4):\n    if number % 2 == 0:\n        print(number, 'even')\n    else:\n        print(number, 'odd')", "1 odd\n2 even\n3 odd", "Using = instead of ==; forgetting to update a while-loop variable; putting too much logic inside one loop.", "1. What does % return? Answer: the remainder.\n2. What does range(3) start with? Answer: 0.", "Make decisions with conditions and repeat predictable work with loops."),
    ("Python Programming", "Functions and modules", "Functions package reusable steps; modules let you organize code across files.", "Define a function with def, accept parameters, and return a value. A return value can be stored or passed to another function. import brings in a module.", "def greet(name):\n    return f'Hello, {name}!'\n\nprint(greet('Maya'))", "Hello, Maya!", "Forgetting return; using global variables unnecessarily; naming a file after a standard library module. Tip: keep functions focused.", "1. What keyword defines a function? Answer: def.\n2. What does return do? Answer: sends a value back to the caller.", "Small functions are easier to test, reuse, and explain."),
    ("Web Development", "Semantic HTML", "HTML gives content structure. Semantic elements communicate meaning to browsers and assistive technology.", "Use header, nav, main, section, article, and footer where they fit. Headings should follow a logical order. Labels should be connected to form controls.", "<main>\n  <h1>Study notes</h1>\n  <article><h2>HTML basics</h2><p>Learn by building.</p></article>\n</main>", "A page with a main heading, an article heading, and readable paragraph content.", "Using div for everything; skipping heading levels; leaving form inputs without labels. Tip: structure first, style second.", "1. Which element contains the primary page content? Answer: main.\n2. Why use labels? Answer: accessibility and clearer form interaction.", "Semantic HTML creates a meaningful, accessible document structure."),
    ("Web Development", "CSS layout", "CSS controls presentation, spacing, and layout. Flexbox is useful for one-dimensional alignment.", "display:flex creates a flex container. gap adds consistent spacing. justify-content controls the main axis; align-items controls the cross axis.", ".card-list {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 1rem;\n  align-items: stretch;\n}", "Cards appear in a row with equal spacing and aligned heights when their content allows it.", "Relying on fixed widths; removing focus outlines; using margins everywhere instead of gap. Tip: start with a small responsive layout.", "1. Which property creates a flex container? Answer: display: flex.\n2. What does gap control? Answer: space between children.", "Use layout primitives, consistent spacing, and flexible widths."),
    ("Web Development", "JavaScript DOM events", "JavaScript can respond to user actions without rebuilding the whole page.", "querySelector finds an element. addEventListener registers a handler. Keep state changes small and update only the required content.", "const button = document.querySelector('#show-note');\nbutton.addEventListener('click', () => {\n  document.querySelector('#note').textContent = 'Keep practising!';\n});", "Clicking the button changes the note text to: Keep practising!", "Assuming an element exists; attaching listeners before the DOM loads; hiding important behavior behind JavaScript only.", "1. Which method listens for clicks? Answer: addEventListener.\n2. What does textContent change? Answer: an element's text.", "Select an element, listen for an event, then make one clear UI update."),
    ("SQL and Databases", "Tables and keys", "A relational database stores related records in tables. A primary key identifies one row.", "Columns describe fields; rows describe records. A foreign key points to a related row in another table. Keep each fact in the table where it belongs.", "CREATE TABLE students (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL\n);", "A students table is created with a unique id and a required name.", "Duplicating the same fact in many tables; using names as identifiers; forgetting NOT NULL for required data.", "1. What uniquely identifies a row? Answer: primary key.\n2. What links tables? Answer: foreign key.", "Good schemas reduce duplication and make relationships explicit."),
    ("SQL and Databases", "SELECT and WHERE", "SELECT reads data; WHERE filters rows before they are returned.", "Name only the columns you need. Use = for exact matches and AND/OR for conditions. Use parameters in application code instead of concatenating input.", "SELECT name, email\nFROM students\nWHERE active = 1;", "Returns the name and email of active students.", "Using SELECT * unnecessarily; forgetting quotes around text; building SQL with string concatenation.", "1. Which clause filters rows? Answer: WHERE.\n2. Why use parameters? Answer: to safely handle user input.", "Read only the fields you need and filter at the database layer."),
    ("SQL and Databases", "JOINs and aggregates", "JOIN combines related tables; aggregate functions summarize many rows.", "JOIN uses matching keys. COUNT counts rows, SUM adds values, and GROUP BY creates one result per group.", "SELECT courses.title, COUNT(enrollments.id)\nFROM courses\nLEFT JOIN enrollments ON courses.id = enrollments.course_id\nGROUP BY courses.id;", "Each course is returned with its enrollment count, including courses with zero enrollments.", "Joining on the wrong columns; forgetting GROUP BY; using INNER JOIN when unmatched rows matter.", "1. Which function counts rows? Answer: COUNT.\n2. Why use LEFT JOIN here? Answer: to retain courses with zero enrollments.", "Join on keys, aggregate deliberately, and verify whether unmatched rows should remain."),
    ("Data Structures and Algorithms", "Arrays and lists", "A list stores values in order and lets you access items by index.", "Indexing starts at 0. append adds to the end. Searching an unsorted list usually takes linear time, written O(n).", "scores = [72, 88, 91]\nscores.append(95)\nprint(scores[0], len(scores))", "72 4", "Using an index outside the list; confusing append with extend; assuming every search is constant time.", "1. What is the first index? Answer: 0.\n2. What is the typical scan time? Answer: O(n).", "Lists preserve order; choose operations with their time cost in mind."),
    ("Data Structures and Algorithms", "Stacks and queues", "A stack is last-in, first-out. A queue is first-in, first-out.", "Stacks use push/pop; browser history is a familiar example. Queues use enqueue/dequeue; print jobs are a familiar example.", "stack = []\nstack.append('A')\nstack.append('B')\nprint(stack.pop())", "B", "Removing from the wrong end; using a stack when order of arrival matters. Tip: name operations clearly.", "1. Which item leaves a stack first? Answer: the last item added.\n2. Which structure models a waiting line? Answer: queue.", "Choose a stack for undo-like behavior and a queue for fair ordering."),
    ("Data Structures and Algorithms", "Searching and sorting", "Algorithms are repeatable procedures. Searching finds values; sorting orders them.", "Linear search checks items one by one. Binary search repeatedly halves a sorted range. Sorting can make later searches faster but has a cost.", "values = [1, 3, 5, 7]\ntarget = 5\nprint(target in values)", "True", "Using binary search on unsorted data; changing a collection unexpectedly; ignoring empty input.", "1. What does binary search require? Answer: sorted data.\n2. What does a sorting algorithm change? Answer: the order of items.", "Understand the input assumptions and trade-offs before choosing an algorithm."),
    ("Data Analysis", "Understanding tabular data", "Data analysis starts by asking a clear question and understanding what each row and column represents.", "A row is an observation; a column is a variable. Numeric, categorical, and date values need different summaries. A data dictionary records meaning and units.", "sales = [120, 150, 90]\naverage = sum(sales) / len(sales)\nprint(average)", "120.0", "Treating an ID as a measurement; ignoring units; answering a vague question. Tip: write the question before choosing a chart.", "1. What is an observation? Answer: one row or recorded case.\n2. Why use a data dictionary? Answer: to document meaning and units.", "Start with a precise question, understand the columns, and check data types."),
    ("Data Analysis", "Cleaning and summarizing data", "Cleaning makes data consistent enough to analyze without hiding important problems.", "Check missing values, duplicates, impossible values, and inconsistent labels. Mean summarizes typical numeric values but can be affected by outliers; median is often more robust.", "values = [10, 12, 12, 100]\nprint(sorted(values)[1:3])", " [12, 12] (the middle values help explain the median)", "Deleting missing rows without investigating; changing raw data permanently; confusing a correction with an assumption.", "1. What is an outlier? Answer: an unusually distant value.\n2. Why preserve raw data? Answer: to reproduce and review cleaning decisions.", "Profile first, document cleaning decisions, and compare summaries before and after."),
    ("Data Analysis", "Charts and data stories", "A useful chart makes a comparison, trend, or relationship easy to see.", "Use a line chart for change over time, bars for category comparisons, and a scatter plot for relationships. Label axes, include units, and avoid decorative effects that hide the message.", "months = ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar']\nvisits = [20, 35, 30]\n# A line chart would show the change across months.", "The chart would show visits rising in February and falling slightly in March.", "Using 3D effects; starting a bar axis at a misleading value; showing too many colors. Tip: title the chart with the insight.", "1. Which chart shows a trend? Answer: line chart.\n2. What should axes include? Answer: labels and units.", "Choose a chart for the question, label it clearly, and state the insight in plain language."),
    ("Artificial Intelligence Fundamentals", "What is machine learning?", "Machine learning finds patterns in examples instead of using only hand-written rules.", "Features are input signals; a label is the expected answer in supervised learning. Training learns from examples; evaluation checks generalization on unseen data.", "A model receives hours studied and practice attempts as features, then predicts whether a student may need support.", "The model produces a prediction such as 'needs review'; it is not guaranteed to be correct.", "Confusing correlation with causation; training and testing on the same records; treating predictions as facts.", "1. What is a feature? Answer: an input signal.\n2. Why use held-out data? Answer: to estimate performance on unseen examples.", "Good ML needs relevant data, honest evaluation, and careful interpretation."),
    ("Artificial Intelligence Fundamentals", "Training and evaluation", "A model learns parameters during training and should be evaluated on data it did not see.", "Split data into training and test sets. Accuracy is correct predictions divided by all predictions, but class imbalance may require other metrics.", "If 90 of 100 predictions are correct, accuracy is 90/100 = 90%.", "Accuracy is 90%.", "Evaluating only on training data; relying on accuracy for rare events; changing the test set repeatedly.", "1. What is overfitting? Answer: memorizing training examples instead of generalizing.\n2. What does a test set measure? Answer: performance on unseen data.", "Separate learning from evaluation and choose metrics that fit the problem."),
    ("Artificial Intelligence Fundamentals", "Responsible AI basics", "Responsible AI considers privacy, fairness, transparency, safety, and human oversight.", "Ask what data is collected, who may be harmed, how errors are handled, and whether a person can challenge an outcome. Minimize data and protect sensitive fields.", "Before using a student-support model, document its purpose, review outcomes across groups, and provide a human review path.", "A safer system explains its limits and gives people meaningful control.", "Collecting data because it is available; hiding uncertainty; automating high-impact decisions without review.", "1. Why minimize data? Answer: to reduce privacy and misuse risk.\n2. What is human oversight? Answer: people can review and correct important decisions.", "Useful AI is also explainable, privacy-aware, tested, and accountable."),
    ("Git and GitHub", "Repositories and commits", "Git records versions of a project. A repository contains the project history.", "git init creates a repository. git add stages changes. git commit records a snapshot with a message. Commit small, meaningful changes.", "git add app.py\ngit commit -m \"Add study notes route\"", "Git stores a commit with the staged change and message.", "Committing secrets; vague messages; editing without checking status. Tip: use git diff before committing.", "1. What does staging do? Answer: selects changes for the next commit.\n2. What is a commit? Answer: a recorded project snapshot.", "Check, stage, review, and commit focused changes."),
    ("Git and GitHub", "Branches and pull requests", "Branches let you work on a change without disturbing the main line.", "git switch -c feature/notes creates a branch. Push it to share work. A pull request asks others to review and merge the change.", "git switch -c feature/search\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Add material search\"", "A new feature branch contains the focused search work.", "Working directly on main; huge unrelated pull requests; merging without tests.", "1. Why use a branch? Answer: isolate work.\n2. What does a pull request provide? Answer: review and a merge conversation.", "Branches support safe collaboration; pull requests make changes reviewable."),
    ("Git and GitHub", "Merge conflicts", "A conflict happens when Git cannot automatically combine changes to the same lines.", "Run git status, open each conflicted file, choose the intended content, remove conflict markers, then git add and commit the resolution.", "<<<<<<< current\nold line\n=======\nnew line\n>>>>>>> feature", "After resolving markers and staging the file, the merge can complete.", "Deleting both versions without reading; leaving conflict markers; skipping tests after a merge.", "1. Which command shows conflicts? Answer: git status.\n2. What must be removed? Answer: conflict markers.", "Resolve deliberately, review the result, and test after merging."),
]
